{%- comment -%}
  The size chart panel, under the description on the product page.

  This replaces the version that read a single shop-wide page: each product
  now carries its own chart, in two metafields
  `scripts/shopify_size_charts.py` writes from `data/size_charts.json` --
  the same charts the WhatsApp bot sends.

    custom.size_chart       the diagram (a file reference)
    custom.size_chart_data  the measurements (JSON)

  Still a native <details>, so it works with no JavaScript, and still using
  the theme's own `.wns-sizechart` classes.

  Both languages show at once rather than switching. The shop's customers
  read Arabic and English interchangeably, often in the same sentence, and a
  chart is a glanceable thing -- a customer looking for "chest" should not
  have to change the storefront's language to find it.

  The closing line is not decoration. Without it customers read a 31 cm waist
  as a body measurement and conclude the trousers are made for a child.

  A product may have the measurements, the diagram, or only one of them --
  a chart uploaded from the dashboard is a picture with nothing published
  behind it, and `wns-boxy-tee` is the other way round. Each part renders on
  its own.

  `page:` is still honoured as a fallback, so a product with no chart of its
  own shows the shop-wide page if one is configured -- and a product with
  neither renders nothing at all.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign chart = product.metafields.custom.size_chart_data.value -%}
{%- assign diagram = product.metafields.custom.size_chart.value -%}
{%- assign fallback = pages[page] -%}

{%- if chart.sizes.size > 0 or diagram != blank or fallback != blank -%}
  <details class="wns-sizechart">
    <summary>دليل المقاسات · Size chart</summary>
    <div class="wns-sizechart__body">
      {%- if chart.sizes.size > 0 -%}
        <div class="wns-sizechart__scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">
                  <span dir="rtl">المقاس</span>
                  <span class="wns-sizechart__en">Size</span>
                </th>
                {%- for measurement in chart.measurements -%}
                  <th scope="col">
                    <span dir="rtl">
                      {{ measurement.label_ar }}
                      {%- if measurement.marker %} ({{ measurement.marker }}){% endif -%}
                    </span>
                    <span class="wns-sizechart__en">
                      {{ measurement.label_en }}
                      {%- if measurement.marker %} ({{ measurement.marker }}){% endif -%}
                    </span>
                  </th>
                {%- endfor -%}
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {%- for size in chart.sizes -%}
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{{ size.name }}</th>
                  {%- for measurement in chart.measurements -%}
                    {%- assign value = size.values[measurement.key] -%}
                    <td>{% if value %}{{ value }}{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}</td>
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </tr>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        Outside the table's branch: a product may carry a diagram with no
        published measurements behind it, which is what the dashboard's
        "upload a size chart" produces.

        A file_reference resolves to an `image` for an image file and a
        `generic_file` for anything else. Only the first understands
        `image_url`; asking a generic_file for one yields an empty src and a
        broken picture, so each is asked for what it can answer.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if diagram.width -%}
        <img class="wns-sizechart__diagram"
             src="{{ diagram | image_url: width: 900 }}"
             alt="{{ chart.title | default: product.title }} — قياسات · measurements"
             loading="lazy">
      {%- elsif diagram.url -%}
        <img class="wns-sizechart__diagram"
             src="{{ diagram.url }}"
             alt="{{ chart.title | default: product.title }} — قياسات · measurements"
             loading="lazy">
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The shop-wide page, only when this product brought neither a table nor
        a diagram of its own -- a product with a chart picture and no published
        measurements (what the dashboard's upload produces) is answered, not
        fallen back from.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if chart.sizes.size == 0 and diagram == blank -%}
        {{ fallback.content }}
      {%- endif -%}

      <p class="wns-eyebrow" style="margin-top: var(--sp-4); line-height: 1.6;">
        Garment measurements in {{ chart.unit | default: 'cm' }}, laid flat — not body measurements.
        مقاسات المنتج مفرود بال{{ chart.unit | default: 'cm' }}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  {%- comment -%}
    Only what `.wns-sizechart` in base.css does not already cover: the second
    language under each heading, and centred number columns so a size reads
    across cleanly.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <style>
    .wns-sizechart__scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
    .wns-sizechart__body th span { display: block; }
    .wns-sizechart__body thead th,
    .wns-sizechart__body tbody td { text-align: center; }
    .wns-sizechart__body tbody th { text-align: left; font-weight: var(--fw-medium, 500); }
    .wns-sizechart__en { font-size: 0.82em; color: var(--text-muted); text-transform: none; }
    .wns-sizechart__diagram {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto;
      margin-top: var(--sp-5); border-radius: var(--r-sm, 4px);
    }
  </style>
{%- endif -%}
